3. Key Difference
Feature	Relational DB	Semantic KG
Hierarchical traversal	Recursive CTEs, manual joins	Property paths (:hasComponent+)
Reuse of rules	Must code per query	Once in ontology, reused everywhere
Temporal reasoning	Possible but coded in SQL	Modeled via temporal ontology + rules
Implicit inference	No (must be explicit query)	Yes (via reasoning engine)
Schema flexibility	Rigid, schema changes costly	Extensible with new classes/relations


When a graph DB helps (vs. pure SQL)

1) Variable-depth hierarchy without pain

Questions like “all descendants of 7100” or “full chain from 7012 up to the top” are single traversals (*.. in Cypher / SPARQL property paths) instead of iterative self-joins or CTE recursion.

Performance usually scales with the number of edges visited rather than exploding join plans.

2) “Where-used” and impact analysis

“If 7003 changes, which top assemblies are impacted?” = reverse traversal.

“Which parts are common between 7100 and 8100?” = intersection of descendant sets—straightforward graph ops.

3) Multi-parent and cross-domain links

Real BOMs have: reuse across products, alternates, supersedures, effectivity, vendor equivalents, service bulletins, quality findings, etc. These create lateral edges that are awkward in strict trees and become join forests in SQL; they’re trivial as edges.

4) Time & configuration reasoning

Model instances (serialised items) and config states over time as edges with validity intervals.

“What was installed on 9000 on 2024-08-01?” = time-bounded traversal over :INSTALLED_AT {from,to} edges. SQL can do it but becomes complex quickly.

5) Provenance & versioning

Attach provenance to edges (source, rule, confidence). Graphs let the relationship itself carry properties—first-class citizens for audits.

6) Graph algorithms out-of-the-box

Cycle detection (data hygiene), path finding, community/overlap analysis of subassemblies, “most reused subassembly” (centrality) without bespoke SQL.

7) Schema agility

New relationship types (e.g., :ALTERNATE_OF, :AFFECTED_BY_AD) are additive; you don’t need schema migrations, join tables, ETL rewrites.

8) Integration surface

It’s easy to connect BOM nodes to PLM/ERP/SCM/maintenance/quality data via edges rather than proliferating foreign keys and bridge tables.
